"Capacity of Lorentzian polynomials and distance to binomial distributions (https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.14406)\nciteturn6academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.9 years ago; Title: Capacity of Lorentzian polynomials and distance to binomial distributions ... In this paper we study the capacity of Lorentzian polynomials.\nTitle: Capacity of Lorentzian polynomials and distance to binomial distributions\nAuthors: Ádám Schweitzer\nDate: Sun Nov 29 17:57:32 2020\n\nIn this paper we study the capacity of Lorentzian polynomials. We give a new proof of a theorem of Br\\\"and\\'en, Leake and Pak. Our approach is probabilistic in nature and uses a lemma about a certain distance of binomial distributions to distributions with fixed expected value.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCapacity of Lorentzian polynomials and distance to binomial distributions (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/346511305_Capacity_of_Lorentzian_polynomials_and_distance_to_binomial_distributions)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 months ago; # Capacity of Lorentzian polynomials and distance to binomial distributions ... In this paper we study the capacity of Lorentzian polynomials. ... The notion of the capacity of a polynomial was introduced by Gurvits around 2005, originally to give drastically simplified proofs of the van der Waerden lower bound for permanents of doubly stochastic matrices and Schrijver’s inequality for perfect matchings of regular bipartite graphs.\n\nPreprint\n\n# Capacity of Lorentzian polynomials and distance to binomial distributions\n\n  * November 2020\n\nDOI:10.48550/arXiv.2011.14406\n\nAuthors:\n\nÁdám Schweitzer\n\nÁdám Schweitzer\n\n  * This person is not on ResearchGate, or hasn't claimed this research yet.\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nRequest file PDF\n\nTo read the file of this research, you can request a copy directly from the author.\n\nPreprints and early-stage research may not have been peer reviewed yet.\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this paper we study the capacity of Lorentzian polynomials. We give a new proof of a theorem of Br\\\"and\\'en, Leake and Pak. Our approach is probabilistic in nature and uses a lemma about a certain distance of binomial distributions to distributions with fixed expected value.\n\n## No file available\n\nImage: Request Full-text Paper PDF\n\nTo read the file of this research,\nyou can request a copy directly from the author.\n\nRequest file PDF\n\n## Citations (0)\n\n## References (11)\n\nResearchGate has not been able to resolve any citations for this publication.\n\nCounting matchings via capacity-preserving operators\n\nArticle\n\nFull-text available\n\n  * Apr 2021\n\n  * Leonid Gurvits\n  * Jonathan Leake\n\nThe notion of the capacity of a polynomial was introduced by Gurvits around 2005, originally to give drastically simplified proofs of the van der Waerden lower bound for permanents of doubly stochastic matrices and Schrijver’s inequality for perfect matchings of regular bipartite graphs. Since this seminal work, the notion of capacity has been utilised to bound various combinatorial quantities and to give polynomial-time algorithms to approximate such quantities (e.g. the number of bases of a matroid). These types of results are often proven by giving bounds on how much a particular differential operator can change the capacity of a given polynomial. In this paper, we unify the theory surrounding such capacity-preserving operators by giving tight capacity preservation bounds for all nondegenerate real stability preservers. We then use this theory to give a new proof of a recent result of Csikvári, which settled Friedland’s lower matching conjecture.\n\nView\n\n\n  * Jan 2020\n  * 821-891\n\n  * Petter Brändén\n  * June Huh\n\nPetter Brändén and June Huh. Lorentzian polynomials. Annals of Mathematics, 192(3):821-891, 2020.\n\n  * Jan 2020\n\n  * Jonathan Petter Brändén\n  * Igor Leake\n  * Pak\n\nPetter Brändén, Jonathan Leake, and Igor Pak. Lower bounds for contingency tables via lorentzian polynomials. arXiv preprint arXiv:2008.05907, 2020.\n\n  * Jan 2020\n\n  * Péter Csikvári\n  * Ádám Schweitzer\n\nPéter Csikvári and Ádám Schweitzer. Short survey on stable polynomials, orientations and matchings. arXiv preprint arXiv:2006.16847, 2020.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Nearly Tight Lower Bound for Matroid Intersection Prophet Inequalities (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.20696)\nciteturn6academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Title: A Nearly Tight Lower Bound for Matroid Intersection Prophet Inequalities ... Our construction also yields an $Ω(d/\\log d)$ lower bound for $d$-single-minded auctions, where buyers request fixed bundles of at most $d$ unit-capacity items.Our construction and analysis build on the \"big-decisions-first\" framework of Rubinstein and Singla (STOC 2026).\nTitle: A Nearly Tight Lower Bound for Matroid Intersection Prophet Inequalities\nAuthors: Dimitris Fotakis, Charalampos Platanos, Thanos Tolias\nDate: Thu Sep 17 17:00:18 2026\n\nWe study prophet inequalities under intersections of $q$ partition matroids, where an online algorithm irrevocably selects elements with independent nonnegative values drawn from known distributions and revealed in an adversarial order. We prove an $Ω(q/\\log q)$ lower bound on the competitive ratio. Together with the known $O(q)$ upper bounds, this resolves, up to a logarithmic factor, the optimal dependence on $q$, an open question posed by Correa, Cristi, Fielbaum, Pollner, and Weinberg (IPCO 2022) and Saxena, Velusamy, and Weinberg (ITCS 2023). Our construction also yields an $Ω(d/\\log d)$ lower bound for $d$-single-minded auctions, where buyers request fixed bundles of at most $d$ unit-capacity items. Our construction and analysis build on the \"big-decisions-first\" framework of Rubinstein and Singla (STOC 2026).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLorentzian Polynomials (https://api.emergentmind.com/topics/lorentzian-polynomial)\nciteturn6search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; Crawled: 2 months ago;   * The Lorentzian signature condition of the Hessian (exactly one positive eigenvalue) connects this theory to that of hyperbolic polynomials, conic stability, and generalized Hodge–Riemann relations. ... Capacity of Lorentzian polynomials and distance to binomial distributions  (2020)\n\n## 5. Capacity, Inequalities, and Combinatorial Applications\n\n  * Capacity inequalities for Lorentzian polynomials generalize classical bounds, such as Newton’s inequalities, to the multivariate setting (Schweitzer, 2020). For a polynomial $\\alpha$α 8 and a multi-index $\\alpha$α 9, $d$d 0 expresses a measure of extremal coefficient growth, and rigorous probabilistic methods connect these bounds to binomial distributions.\n  * Nikolskii-type and Markov-type inequalities are sharp for Lorentz polynomials, controlling $d$d 1 norms and derivative maxima in terms of polynomial norms, with constants improved over Erdős-type bounds for polynomials with zero constraints (Erdelyi, 2014).\n  * In graph theory, transformed independence polynomials become Lorentzian after suitable homogenization, which yields ultra log-concavity and implies unimodality for independence sequences of large classes of graphs (e.g., those produced by the $d$d 2 operator), making progress on longstanding combinatorial conjectures (Bendjeddou et al., 2024).\n\n  * Extension of stability theory to more general dynamical systems using $\\Delta^d_n$Δ n d​2-Lorentzian analysis.\n\nLorentzian polynomials thus unify and extend foundational results in discrete convexity, algebraic geometry, optimization, and combinatorics by encoding log-concavity, negative dependence, and deep topological and geometric structure across these domains.\n\nReferences (13)\n\n1.\n\nLorentzian polynomials  (2019)\n\n2.\n\nLorentzian polynomials on cones and the Heron-Rota-Welsh conjecture  (2021)\n\n3.\n\nLorentzian polynomials on cones  (2023)\n\n4.\n\n$\\mathcal{K}$-Lorentzian Polynomials  (2024)\n\n5.\n\n$\\K$-Lorentzian and $\\K$-CLC Polynomials in Stability Analysis  (2025)\n\n6.\n\nLorentzian polynomials and matroids over triangular hyperfields 1: Topological aspects  (2025)\n\n7.\n\nCapacity of Lorentzian polynomials and distance to binomial distributions  (2020)\n\n8.\n\nInequalities for Lorentz polynomials  (2014)\n\n9.\n\nLorentzian polynomials and the independence sequences of graphs  (2024)\n\n10.\n\nPolynomials with Lorentzian Signature, and Computing Permanents via Hyperbolic Programming  (2022)\n\n11.\n\nDually Lorentzian Polynomials  (2023)\n\n12.\n\nPostnikov--Stanley polynomials are Lorentzian  (2024)\n\n13.\n\nHigher Lorentzian Polynomials, Higher Hessians, and the Hodge-Riemann Property for Graded Oriented Artinian Gorenstein Algebras in Codimension Two  (2022)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSubquadratic-Query Algorithms for Finding Another Maximum Matroid Intersection (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.18199)\nciteturn6academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Title: Subquadratic-Query Algorithms for Finding Another Maximum Matroid Intersection ... Date: Wed Sep 16 06:34:59 2026 ... For the opposite side, where no transposed oracle is available, we give a collective randomized classifier and a deterministic capacity-saturation certificate.\nTitle: Subquadratic-Query Algorithms for Finding Another Maximum Matroid Intersection\nAuthors: Makoto Watanabe\nDate: Wed Sep 16 06:34:59 2026\n\nLet $\\mathcal{M}_1,\\mathcal{M}_2$ be two matroids on a common ground set $V$, given by independence oracles, and let $S$ be a maximum-cardinality common independent set. We study the problem of deciding whether there exists another maximum common independent set $T\\ne S$, and of outputting one when it exists. Writing $n=|V|$ and $r=|S|$, we give a Las Vegas algorithm using $\\tilde O(n\\sqrt r)$ expected independence queries and a deterministic algorithm using $\\tilde O(nr^{2/3})$ queries. As an application, all maximum common independent sets can be enumerated with at most two another-solution calls between consecutive outputs and after the last output; if there are $L$ solutions, exactly $2L-1$ such calls are made. Neither algorithm constructs the exchange graph. Instead, they perform Kahn-style source peeling through two deletion-only data structures. One side uses the heavy/light categorization of Blikstad, van den Brand, Mukhopadhyay, and Nanongkai. For the opposite side, where no transposed oracle is available, we give a collective randomized classifier and a deterministic capacity-saturation certificate.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMeasurable Matroids: Foundations and Min--Max Theorems (https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.16464)\nciteturn6academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Date: Mon Aug 17 12:03:14 2026 ... We prove measurable versions of Edmonds' matroid intersection theorem and the Edmonds--Fulkerson matroid union theorem, together with an attainment theorem for common bases. ... Applied to partition matroids, it gives an exact Hall-deficiency formula for measurable matchings in bipartite graphings and a min--max theorem for continuous bipartite $b$-matchings, with applications to capacity-constrained transport and prescribed cross-sections.\nTitle: Measurable Matroids: Foundations and Min--Max Theorems\nAuthors: Kristóf Bérczi, András Imolay, Ádám Schweitzer\nDate: Mon Aug 17 12:03:14 2026\n\nWe develop a measure-theoretic analogue of matroid theory on standard atomless measure spaces. Motivated by the quotient-convergence framework for submodular set functions, our aim is to identify suitable measurable objects on the limit side of finite matroid theory and to develop their basic structural and optimization theory. We prove that measurable matroids admit equivalent descriptions by independent sets, bases, rank functions, and closure operators. The class includes normalized finite matroids, cycle matroids of graphings, and measurable analogues of partition, nested, lattice path, transversal, and matching matroids. We establish measurable analogues of truncation, elongation, direct sum, duality, and minors. We prove measurable versions of Edmonds' matroid intersection theorem and the Edmonds--Fulkerson matroid union theorem, together with an attainment theorem for common bases. The intersection theorem retains the classical min--max form, with the maximum replaced in general by a supremum. Applied to partition matroids, it gives an exact Hall-deficiency formula for measurable matchings in bipartite graphings and a min--max theorem for continuous bipartite $b$-matchings, with applications to capacity-constrained transport and prescribed cross-sections. We give a rank-expansion criterion under which the supremum is attained. For bipartite graphings, this criterion specializes to the measurable perfect matching theorem of Lyons and Nazarov. For measurable union, the Edmonds--Fulkerson rank formula remains valid. As a main application of measurable union and its attainment theorem, we prove measurable Nash-Williams--Tutte theorems for graphings, characterizing approximate coverings and packings by hyperfinite essential spanning forests and obtaining exact decompositions under strengthened rank inequalities.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n10 Selections : Osaka University 100 Papers (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=b8a14254323070d0b263bed927e95cbaec498ed3&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn6search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Theorem 2 (Fujishige[6]): We have ... G = (V, A) is the underlying graph, c : A → R+ is a capacity ... (d) Entropy functions: Let x1,  x2,… , xn be random variables tak- ... problem proposed by Edmonds-Giles [4] in 1977. ... lems and the matroid-intersection problem.\nmultiple-sink flow network N = (G = (V, A), c, s+, S −), where\nG = (V, A) is the underlying graph, c : A → R+ is a capacity\nfunction, s+ is a source, and S −is the set of sinks. For any X ⊆\nS −let ƒ(X) be the maximum flow value from s+to Xin N. Then\nƒ : 2s−→ R is a submodular function.\n(d) Entropy functions: Let x1,  x2,… , xn be random variables tak-\ning on values from a finite set. Put E = {x1, x2, … , xn}. For any\nX ⊆E define h(X) to be the entropy of X in Shannon’s sense.\nThen h: 2E →R is submodular.\nOne of the most general models of combinatorial optimization prob-\nlems that can efficiently be solvable is the so-called submodular flow\nproblem proposed by Edmonds-Giles [4] in 1977. The submodular\nflow problem includes a lot of graph and network optimization prob-\nlems and the matroid-intersection problem. However, algorithms to\nsolve the general submodular flow problem require an oracle (an effi-\ncient procedure) for submodular function minimization(see [8]). Com-\nbining our algorithm for submodular function minimization and the\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCoordinate-View Confusability Graphs and Matroid Rank Certificates | Zenodo (https://zenodo.org/records/20561410)\nciteturn6search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; # Coordinate-View Confusability Graphs and Matroid Rank Certificates ... Transitive confusability is equivalent to intersection closure of this upward-closed family, equivalently to a finite meet-witness condition on the view list. ... Restricted coordinate ranks, computable by Gaussian elimination on the explicit linear presentation, yield polynomial-time upper-bound certificates for the independence number and Shannon capacity Θ(G) without materializing the vertex set. ... paper2_it_supplementary.pdf md5:fb20ff75923d8b7168de14242386b4af  | 247.3 kB  | Preview Download ...     June 5, 2026\nTransitive confusability is equivalent to intersection closure of this upward-closed family, equivalently to a finite meet-witness condition on the view list. In that case the graph is a cluster graph and its Shannon capacity Θ(G) is determined by the connected components. The meet-witness criterion is decidable by a polynomial-time search over ordered pairs of views.\n\nFor affine state families over a finite field, the coordinate functionals carry a representable matroid. Restricted coordinate ranks, computable by Gaussian elimination on the explicit linear presentation, yield polynomial-time upper-bound certificates for the independence number and Shannon capacity Θ(G) without materializing the vertex set. These certificates bound capacity from above; they are not a general exact-capacity algorithm outside the sharpness classes identified in the paper. Minimal determining coordinate sets are precisely matroid bases, and direct sums give additive rank certificates under block composition. A Lean 4 artifact provides supplementary verification for the cited theorem-level claims.\n\n## Files\n\n### \n\npaper2_it.pdf\n\nPreview\n\n### \n\nFiles (3.7 MB)\n\nName  | Size  | Download all\n--- | --- | ---\npaper2_it.pdf md5:0e9e29c8b9e744c17af6a6b864f1937f  | 650.7 kB  | Preview Download\npaper2_it_arxiv.zip md5:7711febf2afe2d1fc0a52a44adf779d5  | 2.8 MB  | Preview Download\npaper2_it_supplementary.pdf md5:fb20ff75923d8b7168de14242386b4af  | 247.3 kB  | Preview Download\n\n50\n\nViews\n\n76\n\nDownloads\n\nShow more details\n\n | All versions  | This version\n--- | --- | ---\nViews  | 50  | 31\nDownloads  | 76  | 45\nData volume  | 71.4 MB  | 34.4 MB\n\n## Keywords and subjects\n\n  * confusability graphs\n  * coordinate projections\n  * Shannon capacity\n  * strong graph powers\n  * graph coloring\n  * zero-error information theory\n\n#### DOI\n\n#### \n    \n    10.5281/zenodo.20561410\n\n### Markdown\n\nCreated\n    June 5, 2026\n\nModified\n    June 5, 2026----------